course: dictionary
lesson: 6313
title: A place of prayer: the mosque
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A mosque is a building where Muslims go to pray and worship together. Mosque is a noun, so you can have one mosque or many mosques. «A mosque is the Muslim place of prayer; people take off their shoes and pray side by side in rows.» The shoes and the rows are clues: this is a place for prayer, not a shop or a school.
explain: Look for clues in the words around the word. A mosque often has a dome and a tall tower called a minaret, where the call to prayer is made. People wash before they pray, and their shoes come off before they go in.
explain: Respect matters in every place of worship. «In a church, mosque, synagogue, temple or gurdwara, speak quietly, do not run, and follow its rules, such as covering your head or taking off your shoes.» Check it by swapping in your own words: "speak quietly, do not run" still makes sense, so you know what respectful behaviour looks like.
fact: A mosque is a building where Muslims go to pray and worship together.
fact: Before praying in a mosque, people take off their shoes and wash.
fact: The main prayer day for Muslims is Friday.
fact: A mosque often has a dome and a tall tower called a minaret, from which the call to prayer is made.
fact: Other places of worship include the church, synagogue, temple and gurdwara.
exercise: «A mosque is the Muslim place of prayer.» Where do Muslims go to pray and worship together? | A library | A hospital | *A mosque | A cinema
exercise: What do people usually do before they pray in a mosque? | Run around the room | Eat a meal | *Take off their shoes | Watch a film
exercise: What is the tall tower called a minaret used for? | *Making the call to prayer | Storing water | Hanging flags for parties
test: Which day is the main prayer day in a mosque? | Saturday | Monday | Sunday | *Friday
test: Which of these is NOT a place of worship? | Temple | *Library | Gurdwara | Synagogue
exam: In a mosque, which behaviour shows respect? | Chatting loudly during prayer | *Speaking quietly and taking off your shoes | Running between the rows | Touching the old paintings
